  "grammar_notes": [
    {
      "title": "Talking About the Past ('akagadzirwa')",
      "content": "The verb 'akagadzirwa' expresses a completed past action. Shona has two main ways to talk about the past: 1) For actions that happened earlier today or very recently, combine the short subject prefix ('nda-' = I, 'wa-' = you, 'a-' = he/she, 'ta-' = we, 'va-' = they) directly with the verb stem (e.g., 'ndamuka' = 'I woke up today', 'wabudirira' = 'you have succeeded'). 2) For events that happened yesterday or further in the past, insert '-ka-' after the subject prefix (e.g., 'takatenga' = 'we bought', 'vakaenda' = 'they went', 'akadanidzira' = 'he shouted').",
      "rule": "Recent Today Past: [nda- / wa- / ta- / va-] + verb | Earlier Past: [ndaka- / waka- / taka- / vaka-] + verb"
    },
    {
      "title": "Counting & Numbers in Shona ('imwe')",
      "content": "In Shona, number words like 'imwe' always come AFTER the noun they count, and their starting prefix matches the noun's group! The basic number stems are: '-mwe' (1), '-viri' (2), '-tatu' (3), '-na' (4), and '-shanu' (5). For example, when counting people ('vanhu'), you use 'va-': 'munhu mumwe' (1 person), 'vanhu vaviri' (2 people), 'varume vana' (4 men). When counting 'ma-' plurals like 'maminitsi' (minutes) or 'mavara' (colours), you use 'ma-': 'madhongi maviri' (2 donkeys), 'mavara matatu' (3 colours), 'maminitsi mashanu' (5 minutes).",
      "rule": "Noun + Number Stem (-mwe = 1, -viri = 2, -tatu = 3, -na = 4, -shanu = 5) -> vanhu vaviri (2 people), maminitsi mashanu (5 minutes)"
    },
    {
      "title": "Talking About Location: 'pa-', 'ku-', and 'mu-' ('mumusoro')",
      "content": "Look at how location is expressed with 'mumusoro'. Instead of separate prepositions like 'on', 'to', or 'in', Shona attaches one of three location prefixes directly to the front of a noun:\n• 'pa-' means 'at' or 'on' a specific point or surface (e.g., 'patafura' = on the table, 'pamusika' = at the market, 'pasi' = on the ground).\n• 'ku-' means 'to', 'from', or 'at' a general destination (e.g., 'kuchikoro' = to school, 'kumusha' = to the rural home, 'kubasa' = at/to work).\n• 'mu-' means 'inside' an enclosed space (e.g., 'mumba' = inside the house, 'muguta' = in the city, 'mumugwagwa' = in the road).",
      "rule": "pa- = on / at a point (patafura) | ku- = to / at a place (kuchikoro) | mu- = inside (mumba)"
    }
  ],
  "cultural_notes": [
    {
      "title": "The Art of Three-Strand Hair Braiding ('Kurukwa Mamumutatu')",
      "content": "Hair braiding ('kuruka musoro') is both a treasured social tradition and a skilled creative art in Zimbabwe. Styles like 'mamumutatu' (three-strand interlocking cornrows sweeping toward the ears) showcase precision and geometric artistry.",
      "rule": null
    }
  ],
